===Zebak, Guardian of Crondis===
[[Zebak]] is the Guardian of [[Crondis]], Tumeken's avatar of resourcefulness. Zebak is considered the easiest boss in the raid due to his blockable attacks and fairly simple mechanics.

Zebak attacks with all three attack styles, however, his melee attack hits the hardest and also has a chance to apply a bleed that causes the player to take damage if they move. It is possible to flinch the melee attack, but this is highly discouraged as it is possible to get killed in one hit on higher raid levels. Zebak's standard attacks will be indicated by visual and audio cues: his '''magic attack''' spits a pot into the air that splits into red orbs, while his '''range attack''' spits a large rock in the air that splinters into smaller rocks. The <u>magic and ranged attacks can be fully blocked against</u> if '''Quiet Prayers''' are not enabled, whereas the melee can hit through prayer, albeit with reduced accuracy and damage. The attacks will only deal damage right before impact with the player, so the magic and ranged attacks can be easily flicked. Zebak has an attack speed of 7 (4.2 seconds), assuming the path level is not modified.

His Defence can be lowered to a minimum level of 50 from 70, therefore it can be beneficial to use a Defence-draining attack. If the player is ranging, a Defence-draining weapon like a bandos godsword can be beneficial to slightly increase accuracy. The [[twisted bow]] is very strong against Zebak, even at lower levels due to his high magic accuracy bonus. If maging, Tumeken's shadow is all but required, as all other weapons are unlikely to hit as Zebak possesses both a high Magic level and defence.

Once Zebak reaches 85%, 70%, 55%, and 40% health, he will queue up one of two special attacks listed below and alternate between them. Poison and [[Jug (Tombs of Amascut)|jug]]s are always spit out with each special attack. Should Zebak's health fall to the enrage point threshold before a queued attack, it will be discarded.

* '''Great Roar''' - Zebak will throw jugs, poison and two/three rocks onto the arena. Several attacks after doing so, he will let out a great roar, dealing massive damage to any players caught in these soundwaves for three hits. The only way to avoid this attack is to stand behind a rock (up to three tiles behind one), causing the rock to take 50 damage (each rock has 150 health).
** Every pattern that Zebak throws out will always have at least one jug that will create safespot(s).
** Rarely, acid may not cover a tile behind a rock.
** Jugs can be pushed or pulled straight or diagonally, so make use of direction to find a jug suitable to destroy near the rocks. However, be wary of diagonally pushing or pulling jugs into rocks, as indirect hits (jug not on the rock) can cause the water to be applied incorrectly.
* '''Tidal Waves''' - Zebak will throw jugs and poison before slamming his tail, causing boulders to drop from one direction and cause a massive tidal wave to sweep from that side of the arena. Much like [[Galvek]]'s tsunami, players must avoid these tidal waves; getting caught by one will push the player down the direction it was going and deal moderate damage. This will push jugs and sometimes clean up poison splatters on the arena, though this is not guaranteed. If the player is shoved out of the arena from the waves, crocodiles in the waters will attempt to bite them for melee damage, which can be partially blocked with Protect from Melee. The player is also reduced to walking speed and cannot attack while in the water, necessitating the use of the nearby ramp to get back on the main arena.

The poison from these mechanics will splatter on a random tile in the arena and may splatter additional times in a 3x3 radius around the impact tile. The poison pools generally remain in the arena permanently unless a jug shatters near them, automatically dispersing the acid pool. Passing waves pushing jugs can also clear pools. Standing on a poison pool will deal fairly low damage at the start, but will quickly spike to dangerous levels that they will easily kill a player in a few ticks, so it is imperative to avoid them at all costs. Regardless, standing in these pools will also inflict poison status at 2 damage.

The first iteration of Great Roar will always place rocks in front of him, poison right behind them and solves to the sides. The second iteration adjusts the position further east, with solves usually next to the rocks.

Likewise, the first iteration of Tidal Waves always starts to the south, and the second one comes in from the north. Every two raid levels will tighten the gap between each wave by one tile. At raid level 0-1, the gap is large enough for the player to fire from ten tiles away without issue. At raid level 2+, there is a slight chance that it will not be possible to attack, as the safe tile would be eleven tiles away, which would drag the player into a tidal wave if they tried to attack from that tile.

<gallery mode="packed" heights="180" style="text-align:left">
Tombs of Amascut - Fighting Zebak.png|Zebak's roar attack. The player has cleared the acid pools around the rocks and is standing behind them to avoid massive damage.
Tombs of Amascut - Zebak wave attack.png|Zebak's wave attack. The acid pools will clear up as the wave passes by.
</gallery>

During the fight, if the '''Not Just a Head''' invocation is enabled, Zebak will periodically cast a blood spell which will either result in a special version of [[Blood Barrage]] or summons a [[Blood Cloud]]. This barrage attack will always land regardless of the player's defence bonuses. In groups, players must spread apart for this attack, or it will deal increased damage and hit accordingly to the amount of players overlapping with each other. Zebak will usually heal for ~66% of the damage inflicted from the attack if not mitigated. The path levels and whether he is enraged determines whether barrages can be flicked against while also blocking his next projectile attack. 

*For levels 2 and 3, barrage can be flicked anytime during the fight. During enrage, blood barrage will hit you a tick before the next projectile hits.
*For levels 0-1 and 4+, barrage can only be flicked before enrage starts. During enrage, prioritise praying against projectiles as they do significantly more damage.

If a Blood Cloud is summoned instead, they will spawn to either side of the arena by the ramps and will follow a random player upon spawning, dealing 2 damage to itself every tick. If it touches a player, it will siphon their health to prolong itself. In the off chance the blood clouds and the rocks from the roar attack are simultaneously present, players can use the rocks as a [[safespot]] from the blood clouds should their pathing bring them towards the rocks. If no targets are within range when they initially spawn, they will wander about aimlessly and will not aggro even if the player gets close to them, making them harmless. '''Note:''' An ''extremely easy'' way to avoid blood clouds altogether is to simply go to the opposite side of where they spawned and stay on one of the two tiles next to that side's ramp. With the clouds taking 2 damage/tick, the distance the clouds must traverse in order to reach players on these tiles perfectly lines up with the time it takes for the clouds to die on their own. Furthermore, most ranged weapons can still attack Zebak from the ramp tile closest to him.

If '''Upset Stomach''' is active, players will need to be considerate of the jugs to ensure that their AoE can provide safe tiles to walk on. It can be helpful to begin pushing jugs to create safe areas to stand on for the wave attack.

Once Zebak falls below 25% of his health, he will enter an enrage state, increasing his attack speed to 4, or every 2.4 seconds assuming the path level is not modified, and no longer do the roar or wave special attacks. This is indicated with a distinct roar if sound effects are active, alongside his examine text changing. Depending on raid level, gear and overall luck, it is possible to skip the last special attack.

