===Phase 3===
[[File:Tombs of Amascut - Warden floor slam.png|thumb|300px|[[Tumeken's Warden]] uses the floor slamming attack. The player is standing in the correct area to avoid damage.]]
The restored Warden will not use any protection prayers or perform any combat style attacks. Rather, it will raise/slam the ground as its primary means of attack, dealing typeless damage. 

The Warden's defence bonuses are adjusted during this phase, being less resistant to melee and equally weak against both magic and ranged. Its Defence can be lowered to a minimum level of 120 from 150.

The Warden will always raise the floors to its right (east) first, then its left, and lastly slam the ground in both directions. For the Warden's side attacks, players must stand at least one tile left/right from the centre opposite of where the Warden is facing. When the Warden slams in both directions, players must stay in the centre tiles in front of its face. It is best to stay within the three central floor columns to minimize unnecessary movement. Failing to stand in the correct areas for these attacks will result in the player being hit for moderate or heavy damage. Like Akkha's quadrant attacks, the attack takes time to travel, so the player can avoid the attack with late reactions as long as they are 6-9 tiles away from the boss. 

The frequency of the slams depends if '''Overclocked''', '''Overclocked 2''' and/or '''Insanity''' is active; by default, the Warden has an attack speed of 7, and these will decrease them to 6, 4 and 3 ticks between attacks.

[[File:Tombs of Amascut - Warden energy siphons.png|thumb|300px|The player equips their melee weapon to quickly destroy the [[Energy Siphon]]s.]]

At 80%, 60%, 40% and 20% health, the Warden will stop slamming the ground and instead focus its energies, creating a set amount of [[Energy Siphon]]s onto the field. The amount created are dependent on team size, with solo and groups having their own batch. Each siphon is immune to ranged and magic damage, and must be destroyed with melee. The siphons start off with 1 hitpoint, but will increase by 1 when four players are present, increasing another 1 for every two players afterwards for 3 and 4 health at 6 and 8 players respectively. The layout and health is determined by the amount of players remaining in the arena; e.g. if a six-man team fought the boss and one player died, subsequent siphon sets would have 2 health instead of 3. Likewise, if two players remain and one dies, the next set of siphons would use the solo layout instead. There is no delay when attacking the siphon with melee.

Players must force the energies back into the Warden before it charges them for detonation; when a siphon is sufficiently damaged, it will face the Warden instead. If all of the siphons are reversed in time, they will collide into them, dealing damage that scales to a percentage of their maximum health. If the siphons are not reversed in time, the Warden will detonate all of them, unleashing a shockwave from the centre of the arena. This shockwave will deal immense, if not outright fatal damage if caught in it; this damage scales to raid level and is also determined by the amount of siphons that were untouched prior to explosion. Essentially, failing to reverse two siphons in higher enrage levels will result in instant death if the shockwave is not avoided. The amount of time allotted to destroy the siphons is dependent on group size and whether or not '''Insanity''' is active.

It is possible to avoid the shockwave, but this is not feasible and should be avoided for the sake of completion. If the player is looking for the maximum points per raid, they can skull skip, which involves ignoring the mechanic (mostly or completely). Since the shockwave is avoidable, standing sufficiently far enough from the centre and running towards the middle shortly after the explosion will avoid the damage completely. The damage the Warden would have taken from properly clearing the mechanic would not occur, and the player can take advantage of the extra health to get more points. However, this is only feasible on a sufficiently high raid level (at least 400+) and requires good gear, as this will set back times.

During the second and third batch of siphons, a phantom of the bosses from the upper floor will appear and assist it in the fight. Players who face Elidinis' Warden during this phase will get [[Akkha's Phantom]] and [[Kephri's Phantom]], while those who face Tumeken's Warden will get [[Zebak's Phantom]] and [[Ba-Ba's Phantom]] respectively. Any invocations that applied to their original '''will apply''' to the phantom boss, so players should be wary of this prior to the fight start. Often, players will fight Tumeken's Warden, since Zebak and Ba-Ba don't have any invocations that affects them for the Warden fight.

====Enrage phase====
[[File:Tombs of Amascut - fighting Tumeken's Warden.png|thumb|300px|Tumeken's Warden makes its final stand. The floor begins to be torn away, and shadows seen on the ground indicate where red lightning will strike.]]

Upon reaching 5% of its health, the Warden will stop slamming the ground and instead begin to shake violently, obtaining a one-time heal of 20.0% of its maximum health in the process. Its Defence is additionally raised 30 levels to 180, though the cap still remains at 120.

The [[Phantom (Tombs of Amascut)|phantom]] that was observing the fight from behind the Warden will begin to consume the arena, ripping out the furthest row of tiles in the arena periodically, up until the last row of tiles directly in front of the Warden. Instead of slamming attacks, bolts of lightning will begin to strike the arena, indicated with a growing shadow over the target tile. The rate of these bolts are unaffected by invocations. Getting struck by one will additionally deal moderate damage.

The phantom bosses will continue attacking as usual, but will have an increased attack speed with '''Insanity''', which can be further increased depending on the boss's path level, if applicable. Players should be mindful of the second boss, as they will reduce the amount of space to move around and can deal high damage if the player runs into their attacks.

Due to the importance of clearing the phase quickly, players should retain ambrosia, using it if they fall below 50 or less health. Brewing is not recommended, as this will lower stats and likely get the player killed since they already have so much to focus on. If ambrosia is not available, the second best option is to heal with a Keris of the Sun special attack.

If the last row is reached, the player should prioritise standing on larger shadows, as there is a small grace time after it has been struck before it can be targeted again. Keep prayer high at all times, especially if a sun keris special has to be used to heal.

Once the Warden's health reaches 0, it will lose its grip on the arena as the phantom slowly consumes it, ripping it to pieces. A white teleportation crystal will appear from where the Warden once stood, leading players to Osmumten's chamber, where they may collect their rewards.

